## Lore

Sura-kappa are small, monkey-like D-Bees of the rivers, lakes and coasts of Japan. They came to Earth in ancient times and were driven out with the oni, dragons and other monsters by the Japanese gods; since the Coming of the Rifts they have returned and now populate most of the coasts of the Sea of Japan and the East China Sea, with scattered groups across the South Pacific.

They look like hairless monkeys about the size of an eight year old child, with greenish-yellow skin, webbed hands and feet, fins on their forearms and legs, and a bowl-shaped hollow in the crown of the head that they keep filled with water when on land.

Kappa live simply, in family groups of rarely more than 20 that sometimes gather into communities of thousands. They scorn human technology and live as tribal hunters of fish and sea animals, sometimes raiding the shore for livestock or the odd humanoid, travelling up to 20 miles (32 km) inland along rivers. Most are civil and happy to talk with intelligent beings about almost anything.

They do not tolerate intruders in their waters. Sailors, pirates and fishermen find their fish scattered, lines snapped, nets cut and ships run onto rocks, and a truly angry clan will sink the boat and drown the crew. Many clans are honorable; they simply see nothing wrong with eating humanoids or defending their territory. Kappa have also been known to carry lost sailors to shore, warn of predators and storms, and heal the injured.

Their enemies are anyone who invades their territory or attacks them, the Lord of the Deep and its minions, Horune pirates, the Naut''Yll and most sea monsters. They are wary of Lemurians, amphibs and most other intelligent sea life, but not of dolphins and other cetaceans. They have no allies as such and keep mostly to their families. A small number want humanity gone and side with the oni, the Lord of the Deep and other dark forces.

## GM Notes

The book prints no player-character caveat for the sura-kappa, unlike the oni entries around it, and gives it its own experience column (shared with the Oni of the One Hundred). The water-bowl weakness is the race''s defining drawback: a kappa on land whose head-water spills is helpless until returned to water. Average N.P.C. level is 1D4+1.
